Chapter: ProcessExitArity

 `process-exit` was bound twice in the builtin table, ~42 lines apart,
 and the fold REPLACES -- so the second bind silently won. The winner was
 a bare `int-ty-default`, the nullary bare-metal syscall, and the loser
 was `Integer -> [Process] Nothing`, the unary host form that all 53
 transpiler plugs emit. `process-exit 0` therefore did not type-check:
 CDX2001, Integer against Fun.

 Both operations were wanted and they are genuinely different -- free
 this process slot, against exit the program with a status -- so neither
 was deleted. They have two names now: `process-exit` is the unary host
 form, `process-exit-self` the nullary bare-metal one.

 This pins the bind in the CHECKER, where a positive test cannot: a
 program that uses the name where an Integer is required fails before
 emit, and fails only if the unary bind is the one in scope. Under the
 old nullary bind this chapter compiled clean. The positive half lives
 in `codex/test/process-exit-status` now that the unary form has an x86
 emitter.

 It is therefore a discriminator and not a restatement: passing the name
 to `take-int` is rejected exactly when `process-exit` is a function, and
 accepted exactly when it is an Integer.

Section: Entry

  take-int : Integer -> Integer
  take-int (n) = n

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni (show (take-int process-exit))
  end
